# Event Objects

So far we can react to events, but not to the details of them. Sometimes we need more: not just that the user clicked, but *where* they clicked; not just that they pressed a key, but *which* key.

That information arrives in an **event object**, and the browser passes it into our callback automatically. We have been ignoring it, because our callbacks have taken no parameters. To use it, we give the callback a parameter:

```html id=page1
<button>Click me</button>
```

```{javascript autorun}
const button = document.querySelector('button');

function callback(event) {
  console.log(event);
}

button.addEventListener('click', callback);
```
```+id=page1
```

Click the button and look at the object that gets logged. It is full of properties describing exactly what happened.

The parameter can be called anything. `event`, `ev`, and `e` are all common; we will use `event`.

## Mouse Events

Click, `mousemove`, `mousedown`, and the rest all deliver a **mouse event**, which carries information about where the pointer was and what was held down.

Three pairs of coordinates, which differ in what they are measured from:

- **`clientX`** and **`clientY`** are relative to the viewport, the visible part of the page.
- **`pageX`** and **`pageY`** are relative to the whole document, regardless of how far you have scrolled.
- **`screenX`** and **`screenY`** are relative to your physical screen.

On a page scrolled to the top, `clientY` and `pageY` are the same. Scroll down 500 pixels and they differ by 500.

There is also:

- **`button`**, which says which mouse button was used: left, middle, or right. This has nothing to do with the HTML `<button>` element; it is an unfortunate name collision.
- **`ctrlKey`**, **`shiftKey`**, **`altKey`**, and **`metaKey`** (Command on a Mac), each a boolean saying whether that modifier was held down at the moment of the event.

And, on every event of any kind:

- **`type`**, the name of the event, such as `'click'`.
- **`target`**, the element the event actually happened on.

This box reports several of them on every click:

```html id=page2
<div id="box">Click anywhere in this box</div>
<p id="readout">No clicks yet.</p>
```

```{javascript autorun}
const box = document.querySelector('#box');
const readout = document.querySelector('#readout');

box.addEventListener('click', (event) => {
  readout.textContent =
    'type: ' + event.type +
    ' | clientX: ' + event.clientX +
    ' | pageX: ' + event.pageX +
    ' | shift held: ' + event.shiftKey;
});
```
```+id=page2
```
```+css
#box { border: 2px solid #00274c; padding: 40px; text-align: center; }
```

Click in different corners of the box and watch the numbers change. Then hold Shift while clicking.

## Keyboard Events

Listeners for `keydown` and `keyup` get a **keyboard event** instead, with its own properties:

- **`key`** is the key that was pressed, as a string: `'a'`, `'Enter'`, `'Backspace'`, `'ArrowLeft'`.
- **`repeat`** is a boolean: `true` when the key is being held down and repeating, rather than freshly pressed.
- The same `ctrlKey`, `shiftKey`, `altKey`, and `metaKey` modifiers as mouse events.

Here they are on a text input:

```html id=page3
<input type="text" placeholder="Type here">
<p id="key-readout">Press a key in the box.</p>
```

```{javascript autorun}
const input = document.querySelector('input');
const readout = document.querySelector('#key-readout');

input.addEventListener('keydown', (event) => {
  readout.textContent = 'key: ' + event.key + ' | repeat: ' + event.repeat;
});
```
```+id=page3
```

Type a few letters, then press Backspace, then an arrow key, then hold a key down and watch `repeat` turn `true`.

## A Worked Example: Enter to Submit

Here is where `event.key` earns its keep. Imagine a chat box with a Submit button. Clicking Submit should send the message, and so should pressing Enter in the input.

The trick is to write the behavior once, in its own function, and reach it from both places:

```html id=page4
<input id="chat" type="text" placeholder="Say something">
<button id="submit">Submit</button>
<p id="sent">Nothing sent yet.</p>
```

```{javascript autorun}
const input = document.querySelector('#chat');
const button = document.querySelector('#submit');
const output = document.querySelector('#sent');

function onSubmit() {
  output.textContent = 'Sent: ' + input.value;
  input.value = '';
}

button.addEventListener('click', onSubmit);

input.addEventListener('keydown', (event) => {
  if (event.key === 'Enter') {
    onSubmit();
  }
});
```
```+id=page4
```

Type something and press Enter, then type something else and click Submit. Both routes end up in the same `onSubmit` function, so the sending logic exists in exactly one place.

Notice that `onSubmit` takes no parameter. It does not need the event object, so it does not ask for one, and the click listener can hand it straight to `addEventListener`.

## Exercises

1. Both buttons share one listener function. Use the event object to put the text of whichever button was clicked into the paragraph.

    ```html id=page5
    <button id="first">First button</button>
    <button id="second">Second button</button>
    <p id="message"></p>
    ```

    ```{javascript}
    const message = document.querySelector('#message');

    function onClick(event) {
      // Write your code here
    }

    document.querySelector('#first').addEventListener('click', onClick);
    document.querySelector('#second').addEventListener('click', onClick);
    ```
    ```+id=page5
    ```
    ```+solution
    const message = document.querySelector('#message');

    function onClick(event) {
      message.textContent = event.target.textContent;
    }

    document.querySelector('#first').addEventListener('click', onClick);
    document.querySelector('#second').addEventListener('click', onClick);
    ```
    ```+test
    assert('#first').click().run('Could not find the `#first` button', 'Clicked the first button');
    assert('#message').should('have.text', 'First button').run('Clicking the first button should show `First button`. Read `event.target`', 'The first button works');
    assert('#second').click().run('Could not find the `#second` button', 'Clicked the second button');
    assert('#message').should('have.text', 'Second button').run('The same listener should show `Second button` for the other button, which means reading it from the event rather than hard-coding it', 'One listener handles both buttons');
    ```

2. Record the horizontal position of a click, measured from the edge of the viewport, into `lastClickX`.

    ```html id=page6
    <div id="target" style="border: 2px solid #00274c; padding: 30px;">Click me</div>
    ```

    ```{javascript}
    const box = document.querySelector('#target');

    let lastClickX = null;

    box.addEventListener('click', (event) => {
      // Write your code here
    });
    ```
    ```+id=page6
    ```
    ```+solution
    const box = document.querySelector('#target');

    let lastClickX = null;

    box.addEventListener('click', (event) => {
      lastClickX = event.clientX;
    });
    ```
    ```+test
    wrap(lastClickX === null).should('equal', true).run('`lastClickX` should still be `null` before any click', 'Nothing recorded before the click');
    assert('#target').click().run('Could not find the `#target` box', 'Clicked the box');
    wrap(typeof lastClickX).should('equal', 'number').run('After the click, `lastClickX` should hold a number read from the event. Use `event.clientX`', 'Recorded the click position');
    wrap(source.includes('clientX')).should('equal', true).run('Use `event.clientX`, which is measured from the edge of the viewport', 'Used clientX');
    ```

3. A page is scrolled 500 pixels down, and the user clicks something. How do `clientY` and `pageY` compare?

    ```{mcq}
    The user has scrolled 500 pixels down the page and clicks. What is the relationship between `event.clientY` and `event.pageY`?
    correct: Correct! `clientY` is measured from the top of the visible area and `pageY` from the top of the whole document, so once you have scrolled 500 pixels they differ by 500.
    wrong: Look back at the three pairs of coordinates under "Mouse Events" and what each one is measured from.
    [x] `pageY` is 500 larger than `clientY`
    [ ] They are always equal
    [ ] `clientY` is 500 larger than `pageY`
    [ ] Both become negative once the page is scrolled
    ```

